[{:db/id "q-167559/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "7064f9b4-74ef-59a6-b3f4-531bef0a4ea4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167559/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "711754b6-5810-5913-a1d2-33f1d87f386a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167559/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-167559/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167559" :question/id #uuid "51669125-3606-55fa-9be9-54fcf245279c" :question/math-academy-id "q-167559" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/4115f50e563758109358ebde96c41dd7674c9a17e3f0040d4c1b807a053fd394.png)\n\nA broker monitoring a company's stock found that the value of the stock $y,$ in thousands of dollars, can be modeled as the union of three curves corresponding to the functions $f(x)$, $g(x),$ and $h(x),$ where $x$ is the number of weeks since she started keeping track of the stock. The graph is shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/0896ba394361067fd7cfcb57d194d25218a30d64974caf99c0458a6cf27c35d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $f$ is part of the exponential curve $y={5}^{x-a}+b$ shown in the figure above. Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,b\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/37ec9bf31a46129d10531f179f16f1f8bb42b88428652ec02e1022ba958b51d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $h$ is part of the graph of the function $y=5-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).$ Find the exact value of $y$ when $x=10.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5+\\sqrt{3}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/9e8cc0891defe34b22259c4a7b84a467a8fd13474b2518c255d630c346985526.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $g$ is a part of the graph of the function $y=2\\sqrt{x}-1,$ as shown in the figure above. Find the number of weeks $x$ at which the stock price given by this function was $$3,000.$\n\n*Hint: Remember that $g$ is measured in thousands of dollars.*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have to solve $g(x)=3,$ or equivalently, $2\\sqrt{x}-1=3.$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term $2\\sqrt{x}$ by adding $1$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{x}-1 & =3 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $\\frac{1}{2}$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{x} & =4 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ (\\sqrt{x})^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{4}-1 & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ 2\\cdot 2-1 & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ 4-1 & =3\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=4$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $2\\sqrt{x}-1=3$  is $x=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-167559/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9eea0ca-bb7a-5e5c-b2d0-472305f2c490"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167559"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167561/field-1/1c7af01e0b38707f5a31a3b343bdafd556e5a9628313b487eeab6d978c50ba82" :answer/id #uuid "593dc740-d1a1-56ff-a60e-cb82f08e837a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167561/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f5ea1743-84bb-54cc-9a9d-09d18bb24617" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167561/field-1/1c7af01e0b38707f5a31a3b343bdafd556e5a9628313b487eeab6d978c50ba82" :answer-field/choices ["q-167561/field-1/1c7af01e0b38707f5a31a3b343bdafd556e5a9628313b487eeab6d978c50ba82"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167561" :question/id #uuid "018c647d-4b25-55cd-980c-a4e54cfcc993" :question/math-academy-id "q-167561" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/4115f50e563758109358ebde96c41dd7674c9a17e3f0040d4c1b807a053fd394.png)\n\nA broker monitoring a company's stock found that the value of the stock $y,$ in thousands of dollars, can be modeled as the union of three curves corresponding to the functions $f(x)$, $g(x),$ and $h(x),$ where $x$ is the number of weeks since she started keeping track of the stock. The graph is shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/0896ba394361067fd7cfcb57d194d25218a30d64974caf99c0458a6cf27c35d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $f$ is part of the exponential curve $y={5}^{x-a}+b$ shown in the figure above. Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,b\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/37ec9bf31a46129d10531f179f16f1f8bb42b88428652ec02e1022ba958b51d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $h$ is part of the graph of the function $y=5-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).$ Find the exact value of $y$ when $x=10.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5+\\sqrt{3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/9e8cc0891defe34b22259c4a7b84a467a8fd13474b2518c255d630c346985526.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $g$ is a part of the graph of the function $y=2\\sqrt{x}-1,$ as shown in the figure above. Find the number of weeks $x$ at which the stock price given by this function was $$3,000.$\n\n*Hint: Remember that $g$ is measured in thousands of dollars.*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nWhat is the largest union of intervals on which the function describing the stock price is continuous?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\in [\\,\\,0\\,\\,,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $)\\cup $\n\n$($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $9$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $)\\cup $ \n\n$($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $9$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,,\\,\\infty ).$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the derivative of $g(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${g}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We apply the sum, constant multiple, and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{x}-1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{(\\frac{1}{2}-1)} \\\\  & ={x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-167561/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffc1d505-17e4-5b0f-9150-506f9edb3350"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167561"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167562/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "6348fbf8-93a9-5f70-a843-e2fc57e1fecd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167562/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "990b9c95-94f3-5669-99fb-ed9e21ba74fa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167562/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer-field/choices ["q-167562/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167562" :question/id #uuid "507fd8b3-1ad8-5b7c-8d2e-e3c1b501263f" :question/math-academy-id "q-167562" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/4115f50e563758109358ebde96c41dd7674c9a17e3f0040d4c1b807a053fd394.png)\n\nA broker monitoring a company's stock found that the value of the stock $y,$ in thousands of dollars, can be modeled as the union of three curves corresponding to the functions $f(x)$, $g(x),$ and $h(x),$ where $x$ is the number of weeks since she started keeping track of the stock. The graph is shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/0896ba394361067fd7cfcb57d194d25218a30d64974caf99c0458a6cf27c35d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $f$ is part of the exponential curve $y={5}^{x-a}+b$ shown in the figure above. Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,b\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/37ec9bf31a46129d10531f179f16f1f8bb42b88428652ec02e1022ba958b51d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $h$ is part of the graph of the function $y=5-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).$ Find the exact value of $y$ when $x=10.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5+\\sqrt{3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/9e8cc0891defe34b22259c4a7b84a467a8fd13474b2518c255d630c346985526.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $g$ is a part of the graph of the function $y=2\\sqrt{x}-1,$ as shown in the figure above. Find the number of weeks $x$ at which the stock price given by this function was $$3,000.$\n\n*Hint: Remember that $g$ is measured in thousands of dollars.*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nWhat is the largest union of intervals on which the function describing the stock price is continuous?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\in [\\,\\,0\\,\\,,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $)\\cup $\n\n$($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $9$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $)\\cup $ \n\n$($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $9$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,,\\,\\infty ).$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind the derivative of $g(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${g}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWhat was the exact rate of change of the stock's value at week $4?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,$ thousand dollars per week." :question/worked-solution "The function ${g}^{′}(x)$ models the rate of change of the value of the stock, applicable in the domain of $g(x).$\n\nThus, the rate of change of the value of the stock at $x=4$ weeks is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in the stock price was $\\frac{1}{2}$ thousand dollars per week." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-167562/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7df31c9-b27a-5ce2-93c8-178ba96da000"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167562"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167564/field-1/9d39412f98d7a5e8bdbe4ccd74f229950ae0438928595551daf6a86aeba00a0f" :answer/id #uuid "52960447-a4aa-575d-a514-f1086596c54e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}x^{6/5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167564/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c45