 \n                                        \n                                        $)\\cup $\n\n$($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $9$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $)\\cup $ \n\n$($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $9$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,,\\,\\infty ).$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind the derivative of $g(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${g}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nWhat was the exact rate of change of the stock's value at week $4?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,$ thousand dollars per week.\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\nThe broker predicts that, in the future, the stock will have an overall behavior similar to the function $\\sqrt[3]{5x-10}.$ Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{5x-10}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{5x-10}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\infty$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{x}$ equals\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $+\\,\\text{ }C$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\sqrt[5]{x}={x}^{1/5}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/5+1}}{(\\frac{1}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/5}}{(\\frac{6}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{6}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6/5})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{6}{5}{x}^{6/5-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-167564/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85af4ca3-5e66-522a-9e5a-6efb1c7114ba"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167564"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167560/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "ed824653-f388-548b-a4fc-00594c9d0772" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167560/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "fbd589c0-8463-525b-9ade-5b6c691af69a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167560/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-167560/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167560/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "a1742ee9-51f8-5bf0-8ee8-78482a258587" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167560/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "e0248aad-3c93-5c0d-a884-18dbbb181d12" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167560/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-167560/field-2/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167560/field-3/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "f3f0c84e-da73-5936-bc3d-d4fd97bd0478" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167560/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "4fe33ff8-37c6-5773-8771-ea69ec680fd3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167560/field-3/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer-field/choices ["q-167560/field-3/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167560/field-4/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "911da000-0796-5ad4-a36d-11d2d42c5316" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167560/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc447dd5-d18b-5e43-a719-65433edb8a16" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167560/field-4/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer-field/choices ["q-167560/field-4/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167560" :question/id #uuid "ec262840-a281-5092-8f4a-2731c30ab1c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-167560" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/4115f50e563758109358ebde96c41dd7674c9a17e3f0040d4c1b807a053fd394.png)\n\nA broker monitoring a company's stock found that the value of the stock $y,$ in thousands of dollars, can be modeled as the union of three curves corresponding to the functions $f(x)$, $g(x),$ and $h(x),$ where $x$ is the number of weeks since she started keeping track of the stock. The graph is shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/0896ba394361067fd7cfcb57d194d25218a30d64974caf99c0458a6cf27c35d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $f$ is part of the exponential curve $y={5}^{x-a}+b$ shown in the figure above. Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,b\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/37ec9bf31a46129d10531f179f16f1f8bb42b88428652ec02e1022ba958b51d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $h$ is part of the graph of the function $y=5-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).$ Find the exact value of $y$ when $x=10.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5+\\sqrt{3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/9e8cc0891defe34b22259c4a7b84a467a8fd13474b2518c255d630c346985526.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $g$ is a part of the graph of the function $y=2\\sqrt{x}-1,$ as shown in the figure above. Find the number of weeks $x$ at which the stock price given by this function was $$3,000.$\n\n*Hint: Remember that $g$ is measured in thousands of dollars.*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWhat is the largest union of intervals on which the function describing the stock price is continuous?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\in [\\,\\,0\\,\\,,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $)\\cup $\n\n$($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $)\\cup $ \n\n$($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-4}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,,\\,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(0,3)$, $(3,9)$ and $(9,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=0.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $[0,3).$\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on $x\\in [0,3)\\cup (3,9)\\cup (9,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-167560/field-1" "q-167560/field-2" "q-167560/field-3" "q-167560/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbfddefc-d68b-5b1e-9ec5-fd18be204d92"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167560"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167558/field-1/6eb084ad90ab80916bda4a9f74ffdf0240dd7c084313f9bc9838389cec8b77ae" :answer/id #uuid "c3362267-5508-5b69-9b97-e8bb31b1e417" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5+\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167558/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "08068434-977d-52eb-843f-8f938ad330f7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167558/field-1/6eb084ad90ab80916bda4a9f74ffdf0240dd7c084313f9bc9838389cec8b77ae" :answer-field/choices ["q-167558/field-1/6eb084ad90ab80916bda4a9f74ffdf0240dd7c084313f9bc9838389cec8b77ae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167558" :question/id #uuid "f2f8a211-58b3-5945-a90e-d557629feeb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-167558" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/4115f50e563758109358ebde96c41dd7674c9a17e3f0040d4c1b807a053fd394.png)\n\nA broker monitoring a company's stock found that the value of the stock $y,$ in thousands of dollars, can be modeled as the union of three curves corresponding to the functions $f(x)$, $g(x),$ and $h(x),$ where $x$ is the number of weeks since she started keeping track of the stock. The graph is shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/0896ba394361067fd7cfcb57d194d25218a30d64974caf99c0458a6cf27c35d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $f$ is part of the exponential curve $y={5}^{x-a}+b$ shown in the figure above. Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,b\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{2}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/37ec9bf31a46129d10531f179f16f1f8bb42b88428652ec02e1022ba958b51d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $h$ is part of the graph of the function $y=5-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).$ Find the exact value of $y$ when $x=10.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{10\\pi }{3}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10\\pi }{3}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{10\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $y=5-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=5+\\sqrt{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-167558/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c5a9d62-eab8-5f09-aeb7-7a9a1c4e6eb1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167558"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167563/field-1/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer/id #uuid "d8588843-daf7-5a6f-8314-235104a07d1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167563/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b349c41c-0a07-59f7-8be0-8560e0bf0b96" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167563/field-1/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer-field/choices ["q-167563/field-1/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167563" :question/id #uuid "5218dee8-10b1-5da3-9a22-e4d729ae555c" :question/math-academy-id "q-167563" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/4115f50e563758109358ebde96c41dd7674c9a17e3f0040d4c1b807a053fd394.png)\n\nA broker monitoring a company's stock found that the value of the stock $y,$ in thousands of dollars, can be modeled as the union of three curves corresponding to the functions $f(x)$, $g(x),$ and $h(x),$ where $x$ is the number of weeks since she started keeping track of the stock. The graph is shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/0896ba394361067fd7cfcb57d194d25218a30d64974caf99c0458a6cf27c35d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $f$ is part of the exponential curve $y={5}^{x-a}+b$ shown in the figure above. Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,b\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/37ec9bf31a46129d10531f179f16f1f8bb42b88428652ec02e1022ba958b51d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $h$ is part of the graph of the function $y=5-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).$ Find the exact value of $y$ when $x=10.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5+\\sqrt{3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/9e8cc0891defe34b22259c4a7b84a467a8fd13474b2518c255d630c346985526.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $g$ is a part of the graph of the function $y=2\\sqrt{x}-1,$ as shown in the figure above. Find the number of weeks $x$ at which the stock price given by this function was $$3,000.$\n\n*Hint: Remember that $g$ is measured in thousands of dollars.*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nWhat is the largest union of intervals on which the function describing the stock price is continuous?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\in [\\,\\,0\\,\\,,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $)\\cup $\n\n$($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $9$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $)\\cup $ \n\n$($\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $9$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,,\\,\\infty ).$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind the derivative of $g(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${g}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nWhat was the exact rate of change of the stock's value at week $4?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{1}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,$ thousand dollars per week.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe broker predicts that, in the future, the stock will have an overall behavior similar to the function $\\sqrt[3]{5x-10}.$ Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{5x-10}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{5x-10}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $k(x)=\\sqrt[3]{5x-10}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=k(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/fee4cee6127ed4c12bc3c68260a6c21917734a44b60273448e68dd832270b0e5.png)\n\nThe graph shows that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }k(x)=\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-167563/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f945ef94-171c-5c19-adb3-f496408dc31b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167563"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167557/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "e2e76a45-fe89-5dbf-a58a-d04d1080ba92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167557/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4e369fcc-a4b9-54ee-ba63-759925e19a34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167557/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-167557/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167557/field-2/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "cbd04ec5-c9e4-5dec-b2b4-e9b77f047ae0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167557/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "e3bf525a-d2eb-5abc-99ae-5519f29407bd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167557/field-2/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer-field/choices ["q-167557/field-2/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167557" :question/id #uuid "92ce441e-2e3a-56bf-bcce-93d7c875b191" :question/math-academy-id "q-167557" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/4115f50e563758109358ebde96c41dd7674c9a17e3f0040d4c1b807a053fd394.png)\n\nA broker monitoring a company's stock found that the value of the stock $y,$ in thousands of dollars, can be modeled as the union of three curves corresponding to the functions $f(x)$, $g(x),$ and $h(x),$ where $x$ is the number of weeks since she started keeping track of the stock. The graph is shown above.\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946716/assets/0896ba394361067fd7cfcb57d194d25218a30d64974caf99c0458a6cf27c35d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $f$ is part of the exponential curve $y={5}^{x-a}+b$ shown in the figure above. Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n\n$\\,b\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the horizontal asymptote of $y={5}^{x-a}+b$ is $y=b.$ \n\nIn the figure above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Hence, $b=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nNext, we use the point $(3,\\frac{5}{2})$ to determine the value of $a.$\nSubstituting $(3,\\frac{5}{2})$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{3-a}+\\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {5}^{3-a} & =1 \\\\ 3-a & =0 \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-167557/field-1" "q-167557/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa86dd3-3bb3-54d5-8940-5dc07ae4c99f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167557"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
